    "question": {
      "math_academy_id": "q-112427",
      "sequence_position": 2,
      "problem": "Which of the following curves is generated by $y=f(x)$ where $f(x)=(x+4)(3{x}^{2}+1)?$\n\n(*Note: Figures not drawn to scale*)",
      "worked_solution": "The equation is already fully factored. Note that the discriminant of $3{x}^{2}+1=0$ is \n\n$$\nD=(0)^{2}-4(3)(1)=-12<0,\n$$\n\nwhich implies that the quadratic factor has no real roots. So by the zero-product rule, we deduce that the function $f(x)$ has a single root at $x=-4.$\n\nWe also find the $y$-intercept of the curve by plugging the value $x=0$ into the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =(0+4)(3(0)^{2}+1) \\\\  & =(4)(1) \\\\  & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the curve:\n\n- has a $y$-intercept at the point $(0,4),$\n\n- passes through the $x$-axis at the single root $x=-4,$ and\n\n- has no more $x$-intercepts.\n\nBecause the leading coefficient is positive, the graph takes an inverted $\\text{S}$-shape. Therefore, the graph looks as follows:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14240558/assets/fa8a5e889a374aae31d7cf7dd82581b00c4d74abfc08b14721f1da3ea19b2e95.png)",
      "answer_fields_complete": true,
      "result": "Correct",
